============================================================
DATASET INFORMATION
============================================================

Dataset Shape: (20631, 26)
Number of Engines: 100
Number of Sensors: 21
Missing Values: 0

The NASA C-MAPSS FD001 dataset contains 20,631 records
corresponding to 100 engine units. Each record contains the
engine number, operating cycle, three operating settings and
21 sensor measurements.


============================================================
DESCRIPTIVE STATISTICS
============================================================

Descriptive statistical analysis was performed for all 21
sensors. The mean, standard deviation, minimum value, maximum
value and coefficient of variation were calculated.

The analysis shows that different sensors have different
ranges and levels of variation. Sensors with higher standard
deviation show greater variation during engine operation,
while sensors with lower variation show relatively stable
behaviour.


============================================================
CORRELATION ANALYSIS
============================================================

Pearson correlation analysis was performed between all the
sensor measurements.

The correlation matrix was used to identify sensors having
strong positive or negative relationships. Highly correlated
sensors indicate similar variation patterns, while weakly
correlated sensors provide relatively independent information.

A correlation heatmap was generated to visually represent the
relationships between the 21 engine sensors.


============================================================
ANOMALY DETECTION
============================================================

A statistical threshold of Mean ± 3 Standard Deviations was
used to identify unusual sensor observations.

For each engine and sensor, the mean and standard deviation
were calculated separately. Sensor values falling outside the
range of:

Lower Limit = Mean - 3 × Standard Deviation

Upper Limit = Mean + 3 × Standard Deviation

were marked as potential anomalies.

The detected anomalies were used as indicators for further
engineering inspection rather than being directly classified
as engine failures.


============================================================
ENGINE-WISE COMPARISON
============================================================

The sensor measurements were grouped according to engine
unit. Mean and standard deviation values were calculated for
each engine.

The comparison helps identify differences in sensor behaviour
between engine units. Engines showing higher sensor variation
or a larger number of statistical anomaly flags can be
prioritized for further investigation.


============================================================
SENSOR TIME-SERIES ANALYSIS
============================================================

Sensor behaviour was analysed with respect to operating cycle.

Time-series plots were generated for Sensor 2, Sensor 3 and
Sensor 4 for Engine 1.

The plots help identify gradual changes, fluctuations and
unusual variations in sensor measurements during successive
operating cycles.


============================================================
BOX PLOT ANALYSIS
============================================================

Box plots were generated for Sensor 2, Sensor 3, Sensor 4 and
Sensor 7.

The box plots show the distribution, median, spread and
potential outliers of the selected sensor measurements.

This visualization helps compare sensor variability and
identify observations that deviate from the normal distribution.


============================================================
FINAL OUTPUT
============================================================

The analysis successfully completed the following tasks:

1. Loaded and validated the NASA C-MAPSS FD001 dataset.
2. Identified 100 engine units and 21 sensor variables.
3. Verified the dataset for missing values.
4. Calculated descriptive statistics for all sensors.
5. Performed Pearson correlation analysis.
6. Generated a sensor correlation heatmap.
7. Applied the Mean ± 3σ statistical threshold.
8. Identified potential unusual sensor observations.
9. Compared sensor behaviour across engine units.
10. Generated sensor time-series and box plots.

The results demonstrate that statistical analysis and
visualization can be effectively used to study aircraft engine
sensor behaviour, identify unusual observations and support
condition-monitoring activities.
